.TH ACCEPT 2N UniSoft
.SH NAME
accept \- accept a connection on a socket
.SH SYNOPSIS
.sp
.B accept(s, from)
.br
.B int s;
.br
.B struct sockaddr *from;
.SH DESCRIPTION
This call is used to
.I accept\^
a connection on socket
.IR s ;
.I from\^
is a result value indicating the address of the entity which
connected, as known to the communications layer.
This call
is used with connection-based socket types, currently with 
.SM SOCK_STREAM\*S.
.PP
If the underlying communications layer has already made a connection
on the socket, then the call returns immediately.
If no connection has yet been made and the socket is nonblocking (see
.IR ioctl\^ (2)),
then a \-1 is returned and the global variable 
.I errno\^
is set to
.SM EWOULDBLOCK\*S.
It is possible to
.IR select\^ (2N)
a socket for the purposes of doing an
.I accept\^
by selecting it for read, since no data may be read
until the connection completes.
.SH SEE ALSO
connect(2N), select(2N), socket(2N).
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Zero is returned if a connection is accepted; \-1 is returned 
in the error cases.  Some important errors returned in errno are 
.SM EOPNOTSUPP
if the socket is not of a type supporting this operation, and 
.SM EISCONN
if the socket is already connected.
.SH BUGS
This call is provisional and will exist in a slightly different form 
in future releases.
.\"N	 @(#)accept.2N	 5.2 of 7/12/84
